(() => {
 const Q={
 dv01:[['Why does a longer bond often react more to a rate change?','More of its value arrives further in the future, where an extra discount-rate change has more time to act. Coupon and price also matter.'],['Does a negative signed DV01 mean something is wrong?','No. This app reports the gain or loss for an upward basis-point move. A long fixed-rate bond typically loses value, giving a negative sign.'],['Can a swap have zero value and nonzero DV01?','Yes. Fixed and floating legs can have equal current value while reacting differently to a rate move. Net value is not net risk.'],['Would equal bond and swap notionals guarantee a rate hedge?','No. Their cash-flow timing and curve sensitivities may differ. Compare DV01 and key-rate exposures, not just principal amounts.'],['Why hold spread fixed in the DV01 experiment?','It separates discount-rate risk from credit risk. Otherwise a price change mixes two different explanations.'],['Is the rate shock in this chart a curve twist?','No. The flat rate moves in parallel. Real portfolios can retain curve-shape risk even when parallel DV01 is hedged.'],['Can a one-basis-point estimate explain a three-percentage-point move?','Only approximately. The exact curve bends, so a large move can produce a sizeable difference from the initial tangent.'],['Why does the CDS rate response differ from a bond response?','A CDS discounts net protection and premium payments, rather than a promised principal and coupon stream. The sign depends on that net position.'],['Does changing the swap coupon affect rate risk?','Yes. It changes the size of fixed cash flows and therefore the response of their present value to discount rates.'],['What should you ask before comparing two DV01 reports?','Check sign, currency, basis-point units, curve definitions, bump method and whether the measures are parallel or vertex-specific.']],
 cs01:[['Why can a protection buyer gain when spreads widen?','Existing protection becomes more attractive when equivalent new protection costs more, assuming the original contractual premium stays fixed.'],['Does a spread move mean the issuer has defaulted?','No. Spread changes reprice perceived credit risk and compensation. Default is a separate event with a contractual payoff.'],['Why does the bond CS01 usually have the opposite sign from the CDS CS01?','A bond holder bears credit deterioration; a protection buyer purchases a payoff against that deterioration. Their economic directions differ.'],['Can a CDS perfectly hedge every risk in a bond?','No. Coupon, maturity, recovery, funding, basis and default-settlement differences can remain after a local spread hedge.'],['Why does this CDS keep its contractual premium fixed?','Resetting the premium to the new market spread would change the trade being valued and hide the existing contract’s mark-to-market response.'],['What changes when you sell rather than buy protection?','The sign of the position’s value and spread sensitivity reverses. A market move beneficial to the buyer hurts the seller.'],['Why does the negative-spread scenario stop at zero?','The teaching hazard model uses spread divided by LGD. A negative spread would imply a negative default intensity, outside that model.'],['Does a larger CS01 tell you the probability of default?','No. It describes a local value response. It is not itself a probability, rating or jump-to-default loss.'],['What does the gap between exact and linear credit P&L reveal?','It shows how the sensitivity changes as spreads move. A local hedge can drift as the credit state changes.'],['Would multiplying CS01 by a regulatory weight directly give SBM?','Only after checking units. A sensitivity per basis point must be converted to the prescribed absolute-spread units, then netted and aggregated.']],
 delta:[['Why does cash equity follow a straight line?','Each additional pound in the stock price adds a fixed amount to the position. There is no exercise choice to change the slope.'],['Why is a call’s delta often small far below its strike?','A small spot increase may still leave exercise unlikely. The option responds less like immediate ownership of the underlying.'],['Why can a put have negative delta?','A put benefits from the ability to sell at its strike when the underlying falls, so its value generally declines as spot rises.'],['Does delta describe the maximum possible loss?','No. It is a local slope. Large shocks, time changes and volatility changes can produce losses that a single slope does not describe.'],['Can two positions share a delta but react differently to a large move?','Yes. They may have different gamma and higher-order behaviour. Matching the tangent does not match the whole curve.'],['Why can an FX option’s delta differ from an equity option’s?','The foreign interest rate changes the effective carry and discounting of the foreign asset. FX quote conventions also change the units.'],['Does a delta hedge freeze the option’s value permanently?','No. The slope changes as spot, volatility and time change; other risk factors remain after an underlying hedge.'],['Why distinguish position delta from option delta per unit?','A position includes its size and direction. A large holding of low-delta options can carry more total exposure than one high-delta option.'],['What happens to the sign of delta when you short a call?','The short position has the opposite payoff, so its delta is the negative of the long call’s delta.'],['Why compare the forward with the option?','The forward shows nearly constant slope under the selected model, while optional exercise produces a changing slope.']],
 gamma:[['Why is gamma often most visible near the strike?','That is where a change in spot most strongly changes the chance of exercise, so delta changes quickly.'],['What does a fully delta-hedged curve look like near today’s spot?','Its initial slope is approximately flat, but a bend remains. Local neutrality does not remove second-order risk.'],['Why does a short option reverse the curved P&L shape?','Shorting reverses every payoff and sensitivity. Positive long-option convexity becomes negative convexity for the short.'],['Can a long gamma position gain from moves in either direction?','After an initial delta hedge, spot-only moves can produce convex gains. Time decay, hedge costs and volatility changes still matter in actual trading.'],['Why does the second-order line fit better near the starting point?','It incorporates both the initial slope and bend. Higher-order changes become more important farther away.'],['Does cash equity need a gamma hedge in this model?','No. Its value is linear in spot, so its spot gamma is zero. Other portfolio risks can still exist.'],['Why can a gamma hedge based on one option leave vega risk?','Gamma and vega depend differently on maturity and other characteristics. Matching one does not generally match the other.'],['What does the hedge slider remove from the chart?','It subtracts a fixed amount of the initial delta exposure. It does not continuously rebalance or remove gamma.'],['Why is an at-the-money option near expiry sensitive to tiny moves?','A small change can sharply alter whether it expires in the money. The transition in delta becomes concentrated near the strike.'],['Is a quadratic approximation reliable for every stress scenario?','No. It holds the local second derivative fixed. Full revaluation can reveal changes in the curve that the quadratic misses.']],
 vega:[['Why can a call gain when volatility rises without any spot move?','A wider range of future outcomes can increase the value of having a choice about exercise, while the exercise downside remains limited.'],['Can a vanilla put also have positive vega?','Yes. Greater uncertainty can increase the value of the right to sell, just as it can increase the value of the right to buy.'],['Is a one-volatility-point move the same as a one-percent relative move?','No. Moving from 20% to 21% is one volatility point, but a 5% relative increase. Units matter before multiplying by vega.'],['Why does a short option usually have negative vega?','The holder benefits from the valuable exercise choice; the writer has sold that choice and bears the opposite volatility exposure.'],['Why does time remaining matter for volatility sensitivity?','More time gives uncertainty more opportunity to influence possible exercise outcomes, although moneyness and carry affect the exact relationship.'],['Does vega predict the next change in implied volatility?','No. Vega measures the response if volatility changes. It is not a forecast of whether volatility will rise or fall.'],['Why hold the strike and spot fixed while exploring vega?','It isolates volatility risk. Moving several factors at once makes it harder to identify what caused the price change.'],['Can two volatility surfaces with the same headline volatility create different risk?','Yes. Real options depend on strike and expiry-specific implied volatilities. A single flat-volatility input suppresses smile and term-structure risk.'],['Why can the vega line drift from exact repricing for a large shock?','Vega itself changes with volatility. A first-order estimate freezes its initial value and misses that evolution.'],['Is the displayed vega already the FRTB vega sensitivity?','No. This app reports value per volatility point. FRTB sensitivity uses the prescribed derivative with respect to volatility multiplied by implied volatility.']],
 curvature:[['Why remove the delta effect from a stress revaluation?','It separates the first-order response already described by delta from the remaining nonlinear value change.'],['Why does the curvature residual use a minus sign?','The displayed CVR convention turns a nonlinear P&L loss into a positive risk amount. A nonlinear gain therefore produces a negative residual.'],['Can a long vanilla option have zero standalone curvature loss?','Yes. Its convex residual can be a gain in both directions, producing negative CVRs and a zero floor for the isolated example.'],['Does zero standalone curvature loss mean the option needs no capital?','No. Delta, vega, issuer default and portfolio effects may still matter. A zero in one layer is not a zero total.'],['Why calculate both an upward and a downward shock?','Nonlinear instruments can respond differently in the two directions. A single shock can miss the more adverse residual.'],['How does gamma differ from the full curvature experiment?','Gamma measures the bend at the starting point. Full curvature reprices at a finite shocked market state and can cross a changing shape.'],['Can a bond have mathematical convexity without mandatory option curvature treatment?','Yes. Mathematical nonlinearity and regulatory curvature scope are different questions. FRTB scope and any elected treatment must be checked separately.'],['Why are the shock controls not labelled prescribed risk weights?','They are user-selected experiments. Actual FRTB shocks depend on the risk factor and prescribed class rules.'],['What changes if you reverse a long option into a short?','The nonlinear gains become losses and the CVR signs reverse, making the standalone loss floor potentially positive.'],['Can you sum these standalone floors to calculate portfolio curvature capital?','No. Regulatory aggregation uses net up/down CVRs, bucket rules, prescribed correlations and scenario aggregation, with restrictions on recognising gains.']]
 };
 window.LEARNING_CONTENT=Object.fromEntries(FRTB_LESSONS.map(l=>[l.id,{intuition:[l.simple,'Use the exact curve as the behaviour of the selected teaching model. The tangent answers a local question; the quadratic adds one more piece of shape. Change one control, predict the sign first, then explain why the curves separate.'],questions:Q[l.id]}]));
})();
